    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i've heard people talk about CNS fatigue a lot when it comes to plateaus. can you explain specifically how the central nervous system plays into this? like, why does a deload help the brain/nerves part of the squat specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The central nervous system (CNS) plays a crucial role in plateaus due to its adaptation mechanism to repetitive motor patterns. When an individual performs a squat repeatedly, their neural pathways, which are the \"wiring\" of the brain and spinal cord, become fatigued and adapt to the stimulus. This can lead to a decrease in neural drive to the muscles, causing strength gains to plateau. A deload helps alleviate CNS fatigue by giving the neural pathways a break, allowing them to recover and adapt, and increasing the neural drive to the muscles again when the program is reintroduced.",
      "source": "target",
